      ],
      "default": "Curved image only"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The rectangle S in the uv-plane",
      "narration": "On the left, the coordinate lines u = u₀, u = u₀ + Δu, v = v₀, v = v₀ + Δv cut out a plain rectangle S of area ΔuΔv. Nothing curved here — this is the easy domain we get to integrate over.",
      "state": {
        "shrinkRectangle": 0.8,
        "showParallelogram": "Curved image only"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "T bends the four edges",
      "narration": "The transformation x = u cos v, y = u sin v sends each straight edge of S to a coordinate curve: the u = const edges become circular arcs and the v = const edges become rays. The image R is a curved quadrilateral.",
      "state": {
        "shrinkRectangle": 0.8,
        "showParallelogram": "Curved image only"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move S — the distortion is local",
      "narration": "Drag S to a different corner of the uv-plane. The same formula stretches the rectangle by different amounts depending on where it sits, so the area factor must be a function of (u,v), not a constant.",
      "state": {
        "shrinkRectangle": 0.8,
        "showParallelogram": "Curved image only"
      }
    },
